## Proof

The integrand is
$$f(x) = \frac{1}{x+(a-ib)}\cdot\frac{1}{\exp(-x/c)-1}.$$

**Poles of the second factor.** The equation $\exp(-x/c)=1$ holds iff $-x/c=2\pi i n$, $n\in\mathbb{Z}$, i.e. $x=-2\pi i c n$. Since $n$ ranges over all of $\mathbb{Z}$, the set $\{-2\pi i c n:n\in\mathbb{Z}\}$ equals $\{2\pi i c n:n\in\mathbb{Z}\}$, so the poles of $\frac{1}{\exp(-x/c)-1}$ are exactly the points $x_n=2\pi i c n$.

**Nature of these poles.** Set $w=x-x_n=x-2\pi i c n$. Then $-x/c=-w/c-2\pi i n$, hence $\exp(-x/c)=\exp(-w/c)$. Expanding,
$$\exp(-w/c)-1=-\frac{w}{c}+\frac{w^2}{2c^2}-\frac{w^3}{6c^3}+\cdots=-\frac{w}{c}\Bigl(1-\frac{w}{2c}+\frac{w^2}{6c^2}-\cdots\Bigr).$$
Therefore
$$\frac{1}{\exp(-x/c)-1}=\frac{1}{\exp(-w/c)-1}=-\frac{c}{w}\cdot\frac{1}{1-\frac{w}{2c}+\cdots}=-\frac{c}{w}-\frac{1}{2}-\frac{w}{12c}+O(w^2),$$
which is precisely the given Laurent expansion. This confirms that $\frac{1}{\exp(-x/c)-1}$ has a **simple pole** at each $x_n=2\pi i c n$ with residue $-c$ (the coefficient of $(x-x_n)^{-1}$).

**Poles of the first factor.** $\frac{1}{x+(a-ib)}$ has a single simple pole at $x=-(a-ib)=-a+ib$.

**Product at $x_n=2\pi i c n$.** Assume $c\neq 0$ and that the two pole sets do not coincide, i.e.
$$x_n+(a-ib)=2\pi i c n+a-ib\neq 0 \quad\text{for all }n\in\mathbb{Z}.$$
(This excludes the degenerate case where $-a+ib=2\pi i c n$ for some $n$, which would merge the two simple poles into a double pole.)

Under this assumption, $\frac{1}{x+(a-ib)}$ is analytic and non-vanishing at $x=x_n$. The product of an analytic non-vanishing function with a function having a simple pole yields a simple pole. Explicitly, near $x=x_n$:
$$f(x)=\frac{1}{x+(a-ib)}\Bigl(-\frac{c}{x-x_n}-\frac{1}{2}-\cdots\Bigr).$$
Since $\frac{1}{x+(a-ib)}$ is analytic at $x_n$, its value there is $\frac{1}{x_n+(a-ib)}=\frac{1}{2\pi i c n+a-ib}$, so
$$f(x)=-\frac{c}{(2\pi i c n+a-ib)}\cdot\frac{1}{x-x_n}+\text{analytic part}.$$

**Residue.** The residue of $f$ at $x=x_n=2\pi i c n$ is
$$\operatorname{Res}_{x=x_n}f(x)=\frac{-c}{2\pi i c n+a-ib}.$$

This is **non-zero** provided $c\neq 0$ and $2\pi i c n+a-ib\neq 0$, which are exactly our assumptions.

**Conclusion.** Each point $x=2\pi i c n$ ($n=0,\pm1,\pm2,\ldots$) is a **simple pole** of the integrand, with **non-zero residue**
$$\operatorname{Res}_{x=2\pi i c n} f=\frac{-c}{a-ib+2\pi i c n},$$
provided $c\neq 0$ and no coincidence $a-ib+2\pi i c n=0$ occurs (which would instead produce a double pole). Under the generic (non-degenerate) conditions on $a,b,c$, the answer is **yes**: the points are simple poles with non-zero residues.

